module gunbc.spark.pxe_rescue_observation

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, Nat }
import std.measure { Second, second }
import extdeps.time.rfc3339 { Rfc3339Timestamp }
import extdeps.systems.nvidia_dgx_spark_pxe {
  spark_pxe_bootloader_filename,
}
import extdeps.os.ubuntu_kernel_nic_coverage {
  PciVendorId,
  PciDeviceId,
}

// SPARK-PXE-RESCUE-0 (2026-09-05). WHAT THIS REPOSITORY ACTUALLY DID TO PUT ONE PHYSICAL
// SPARK UNIT (spark-2196) ON THE NETWORK THROUGH PXE AND INSTALL AN OS ON IT, as opposed to
// what NVIDIA's documentation says should work.
//
// THE LAYER BOUNDARY IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS MODULE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM
// extdeps.systems.nvidia_dgx_spark_pxe. That module owns the vendor's DOCUMENTED PXE surface
// and is cited to docs.nvidia.com/dgx/dgx-spark/pxe.html. Everything here is a RECEIPT
// PRODUCED BY US by driving one physical unit, and DESIGN section 3 (external upstream
// decomposition) is explicit that observations produced by this repository are receipts in
// the observing layer, never facts authored into the observed upstream's module. Writing an
// observed measurement into the extdeps module would make our measurement masquerade as
// vendor documentation, and the next reader could not tell which claims NVIDIA stands behind.
//
// THE OBSERVED SUBJECT IS ONE UNIT AT ONE MOMENT (spark-2196, 2026-09-05), and every row
// below is scoped to that. A second unit, a firmware revision, or a different regional image
// may present different behaviour; this module claims nothing about them.
//
// SEVEN INDEPENDENT RECEIPTS ARE BELOW, each carrying its own observation context.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECEIPT 1: NIC identity 10ec:8127 / r8169
//
// The DGX Spark contains a Realtek Ethernet controller at PCI vendor 10ec device 8127,
// driven by the r8169 kernel module. This NIC is NOT claimed by Ubuntu 6.8 (the Fast OS
// Recovery kernel), so a 6.8-image PXE boot succeeds (DHCP lease, TFTP transfer, kernel
// loads) and then the system has NO NETWORK after boot — the interface is unclaimed.
// Ubuntu 6.17 and later claim it.
//
// grep over the full dag/ tree returns no match for either "8127" or "r8169" as of the
// observation date, confirming this PCI identity is unmodelled anywhere.
//
// LOAD-BEARING CONSEQUENCE: a PXE image based on Ubuntu 6.8 will appear to succeed at
// every pre-boot step and then silently produce an unnetworked system. The NIC identity
// must be modelled before any PXE plan can distinguish "kernel version supports NIC" from
// "kernel version does not".

data pxe_rescue_observation_timestamp: Rfc3339Timestamp =
  "2026-09-05T14:30:00Z" as Rfc3339Timestamp

data spark_2196_pci_vendor: PciVendorId = "10ec" as PciVendorId

data spark_2196_pci_device: PciDeviceId = "8127" as PciDeviceId

data spark_2196_nic_kernel_module: NonEmptyStr = "r8169" as NonEmptyStr

// Annotation: Ubuntu 6.8 does not claim 10ec:8127; 6.17 and later do.
// The kernel coverage fact is modelled in extdeps.os.ubuntu_kernel_nic_coverage
// and consumed by dgx_pxe_boot.dag via r8169_claims_pci_device.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECEIPT 2: Stock arm64 Linux resets the GB10 after ~90s
//
// Booting a generic arm64 distro kernel (not the NVIDIA-provided Fast OS or DGX OS image)
// on spark-2196 causes the Grace Hopper GB10 module to reset the system approximately 90
// seconds after boot. The exact trigger is not isolated, but the observation is reliable:
// three attempts, all reset at ~90s.
//
// THE ROOT CAUSE OF THIS RESET IS NOT THE IMAGE KIND ITSELF. The generic-distro kernel
// tested (Ubuntu 6.8 GA) lacks the r8169 alias for 10ec:8127 (see Receipt 1), and the
// reset may be a systemd/journalled timeout or a GPU watchdog reacting to an unnetworked
// system — neither was isolated. SparkPxeBootImageKind is a closed sum over the two
// NVIDIA-provided constructors (FastOsRecoveryTarball | DgxOsIsoImage), so no value can
// inhabit a "generic-distro" arm and the model refuses images via the existing NIC
// coverage check (UnattendedPxeBootKernelDoesNotClaimNic) rather than by image-kind
// discrimination. See dag/gunbc/spark/dgx_pxe_boot.dag lines 406-416 for the check.

data spark_2196_generic_distro_reset_observation_count: Nat = 3

data spark_2196_generic_distro_reset_duration: Second = second(count: 90)

// Annotation: Three trials, all GB10 reset at approximately 90s post-boot. Root cause not isolated.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECEIPT 3: Secure Boot need not be disabled — BootloaderEnrolledForPxe suffices,
//            but the actual bootloader filename differs from the documented one.
//
// NVIDIA's documented PXE bootloader filename is "grubnetaa64.efi.signed". The
// bootloader that actually booted spark-2196 via PXE was named "bootaa64.efi". This
// is the filename the DHCP server must offer as the bootfile, and the TFTP server
// must serve at that path.
//
// SparkPxeSecureBootPrerequisite already models both alternatives (SecureBootDisabledForPxe
// and BootloaderEnrolledForPxe { bootloader }). The mismatch is between the cited
// constant spark_pxe_bootloader_filename ("grubnetaa64.efi.signed") and the observed
// working filename ("bootaa64.efi"). This matters because:
//
//   (a) the DHCP bootfile offer must match what TFTP actually serves, and
//   (b) the instruction text in the human-intervention carrier cites the documented
//       filename, so an operator following it verbatim would serve the wrong file.
//
// Reconciliation belongs in the dgx_pxe_boot model, not here. This observation
// module only records what filename was observed working.

// Observed bootloader filename. The documented value spark_pxe_bootloader_filename
// is "grubnetaa64.efi.signed"; the observed working value on spark-2196 was "bootaa64.efi".
// A DHCP server offering only the documented filename will fail to netboot this unit.

data spark_2196_pxe_bootloader_filename_observed: NonEmptyStr =
  "bootaa64.efi" as NonEmptyStr

data spark_2196_pxe_bootloader_filename_documented: NonEmptyStr =
  spark_pxe_bootloader_filename

// The existing BootloaderEnrolledForPxe variant has a bootloader: NonEmptyStr field.
// The gap is that the model's only concrete bootloader value (spark_pxe_bootloader_filename)
// names "grubnetaa64.efi.signed" while the observed working value is "bootaa64.efi".
// The bootloader enrolled in Secure Boot must match the bootloader served via TFTP.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECEIPT 4: Boot order is NOT a fleet invariant.
//
// Four Spark units (serial numbers observed during the 2026-09-05 standup) were
// installed with identical procedures and have DIFFERENT boot orders:
//   - 4 units: PXE first in order
//   - spark-2196: ubuntu-first in order
//
// This is EVIDENCE FOR the existing DeviceOnce framing of human interventions in
// dgx_pxe_boot.dag. The DeviceOnce frequency and NoDischargeSiteModeled completion
// evidence are correct: boot order is set once per unit during installation, is not
// reverted by the software stack, and no modelled site exists to read it back under
// programmatic control.
//
// It is also evidence AGAINST modelling a fleet-level "default boot order" in
// extdeps. An operator intending to PXE-boot a Spark unit must verify that unit's
// boot order at console before relying on PXE, because the fleet invariant does
// not exist.

data spark_2196_boot_order: NonEmptyStr = "UbuntuFirst" as NonEmptyStr

data fleet_boot_order_pxe_first_count: Nat = 4

data fleet_boot_order_ubuntu_first_count: Nat = 1

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECEIPT 5: Chainloading local GRUB is defeated by timeout=0.
//
// When PXE first loads GRUB from TFTP, the default grub.cfg on the TFTP server
// delegates to chainloading the local disk's GRUB via:
//   search --file /boot/grub/grub.cfg --set=root
//   chainloader ($root)/EFI/...
//
// This chainload fails when the local GRUB has timeout=0 (the Ubuntu DGX OS default
// installer setting), because local GRUB immediately proceeds to boot its default
// entry without waiting for keyboard input and never provides a PXE-reachable menu.
//
// The working construction observed on spark-2196 avoids chainloading entirely.
// Instead, the PXE GRUB config directly boots the local installation:
//   search --file /boot/grub/grub.cfg --set=root
//   probe --set=uu --fs-uuid $root
//   linux /boot/vmlinuz-... root=UUID=$uu
//   initrd /boot/initrd.img-...
//
// This is relevant to the dgx_pxe_boot model because the existing
// spark_unattended_image_boot function constructs a boot chain for unattended
// INSTALL, not for RESCUE. A rescue scenario that needs to fall through to the
// installed OS must use the direct-boot construction above, not a GRUB chainload.

data spark_2196_grub_timeout_zero_defeats_chainload: NonEmptyStr =
  "timeout=0 causes local GRUB to boot default entry immediately; chainload never pauses for input" as NonEmptyStr

data spark_2196_local_boot_rescue_construction: NonEmptyStr =
  "search --file /boot/grub/grub.cfg --set=root ; probe --set=uu --fs-uuid $root ; linux ... root=UUID=$uu" as NonEmptyStr

// The direct-boot construction requires knowing the root filesystem UUID at
// PXE-GRUB-config-authoring time, so it is site-specific rather than a fleet-
// invariant TFTP template. This observation does not produce a general-purpose
// construction; it only records that chainloading with timeout=0 fails and that
// the direct-boot construction works.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECEIPT 6: IsolatedProvisioningSegment with native DHCP was sufficient.
//
// spark-2196 was PXE-booted using an isolated provisioning VLAN with native DHCP
// (the router's own DHCP server configured with a static reservation and bootfile
// option). ProxyDHCP was not required.
//
// This confirms that the existing PxeNetworkIsolation variant
// IsolatedProvisioningSegment is correct and sufficient for at least one real
// unit. The ProxyDhcpPxeOnly variant exists in the model and may be needed for
// other network topologies, but is not required for spark-2196.

data spark_2196_pxe_isolation: NonEmptyStr =
  "IsolatedProvisioningSegment" as NonEmptyStr

data spark_2196_pxe_dhcp_direction_fact: NonEmptyStr =
  "NativeDhcp" as NonEmptyStr

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RECEIPT 7: The actual fault mode that bricked spark-2196.
//
// spark-2196 was found bricked because an INTERRUPTED SETUP left two coupled
// symptoms:
//
//   (a) Kernel 6.17.0-1032-nvidia was installed with NO initramfs built. The
//       grub-install step succeeded (updating BootOrder — see symptom b) but
//       the initramfs-tools hook did not run before the setup process was
//       interrupted. The default boot entry therefore kernel-panics with
//       'VFS: Unable to mount root fs on unknown-block(0,0)'.
//
//   (b) grub-install rewrites UEFI BootOrder, promoting ubuntu ahead of PXE.
//       The five-unit efibootmgr survey (Receipt 4) shows spark-2196 is the
//       only unit with UbuntuFirst boot order, confirming that grub-install
//       writes BootOrder on install and later setup steps do not revert it.
//
// RECOVERY PATH: boot the OLDER kernel whose initramfs is intact. spark-2196
// had 6.11.0-1014-nvidia still present with a complete initramfs. Selecting
// it via the PXE GRUB rescue entry (see Receipt 5 construction) restored the
// system without reinstalling.
//
// LOAD-BEARING: a unit whose ONLY kernel is missing its initramfs is not
// recoverable this way and MUST go through the unattended install path. The
// kernel image and initramfs version must be discovered at PXE-GRUB-config-
// authoring time, making the rescue entry site-specific.

data spark_2196_broken_kernel_label: NonEmptyStr =
  "6.17.0-1032-nvidia" as NonEmptyStr

data spark_2196_recovery_kernel_label: NonEmptyStr =
  "6.11.0-1014-nvidia" as NonEmptyStr

// Annotation: 6.17.0-1032-nvidia was installed without initramfs; grub-install
// succeeded before the setup process was interrupted.
// Annotation: 6.11.0-1014-nvidia has complete initramfs built during an earlier
// setup phase.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SEPARATE, NOT PXE: OOBE radio constraint.
//
// The OOBE wizard is served by the unit's OWN ACCESS POINT during first boot.
// Driving it requires a radio associated with that AP. This was discovered during
// the 2026-09-02 OOBE observation session (SPARK-OOBE-OBSERVE-0) and confirmed
// during the 2026-09-05 standup: NO HOST on the fleet has both a browser capable
// of rendering the SPA and a radio reachable from the Spark unit's AP.
//
// This constraint belongs in oobe_execution_placement, not here. That module
// already models the composition of renderer, relay, and route. The AP-as-origin
// fact is modelled in oobe_screen_observation as dgx_spark_0c75_oobe_account_run's
// access point SSID.
//
// No duplicate is authored here.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED.
//
// These facts are deliberately NOT claimed by this module and must not be treated
// as if they were:
//
// (a) Unattended install has NEVER been executed. The functions
//     spark_fastos_unattended_kernel_cmdline and spark_dgx_os_unattended_kernel_cmdline
//     in extdeps.systems.nvidia_dgx_spark_pxe are modelled intentions cited to
//     NVIDIA's documentation, not receipts produced by this repository.
//
// (b) Factory firmware defaults are unknown. All observations above were made on
//     a unit in POST-INSTALL state. Which settings differ from factory defaults has
//     not been measured.
//
// (c) The OOBE radio requirement is inferred from the 2026-09-02 capture method
//     (a host associated with the unit's own AP), not re-established by independent
//     measurement during this session.
//
// (d) The reset mechanism (Receipt 2) trigger is unisolated. The observation is
//     three trials producing the same symptom; the root cause (GPU watchdog, thermal,
//     power-state handshake, kernel-NIC-mismatch timeout, or other) has not been
//     identified. The generic-distro reset is not modelled as a refusable image-kind
//     arm (SparkPxeBootImageKind is a closed sum over NVIDIA constructors); the
//     existing NIC coverage check (UnattendedPxeBootKernelDoesNotClaimNic) is the
//     modelled refusal path.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONSUMERS: who references these receipts.
//
// - dgx_pxe_boot.dag cites spark_2196_pxe_bootloader_filename_observed (Receipt 3)
//   in spark_pxe_firmware_enablement_intervention() instruction text and in the
//   bootloader-name-equality-removal comment at spark_pxe_firmware_gaps.
// - dgx_pxe_boot.dag cites spark_2196_pci_vendor and spark_2196_pci_device (Receipt 1)
//   in the NIC coverage check at admit_spark_unattended_pxe_boot and in the
//   UnattendedPxeBootKernelDoesNotClaimNic refusal reason comment.
// - dgx_pxe_boot.dag cites Receipt 2 in the UnattendedPxeBootKernelDoesNotClaimNic
//   refusal reason comment.
// - oobe_execution_placement.dag already owns the radio-constraint composition;
//   no citation needed here.
